    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thx. i think it might be that capacitor thing. do i just buy a generic one or do i need a specific part? also does the humming sound sound like something specific or just a general electrical thing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A capacitor is sized to the unit’s specs, so you’ll need the exact micro‑farad (µF) rating and voltage (usually printed on the old one or in the service manual). Grab a “hard start” or “run” capacitor that matches those numbers; a generic “universal” part can work, but it must have the same rating. The humming you hear is the motor’s magnetic field trying to start – it’s a steady, low‑pitched buzz, not the whir of a spinning fan. If the hum continues after you install a correctly‑rated capacitor, the motor may be dead or the fan clutch is seized.",
      "source": "target",
